COMMIT 47c9b857664c33e8b3471c9ace94113b60a86d2a
fix(core): the recorder's own failure can no longer replace the throwable it rethrows

FIX-133 widened this catch to Throwable so a step that threw an Error would
leave a FAILED row. The review that passed it found what that exposed: the
catch records the row and only then rethrows, so a recording that throws
replaces the throwable it was about to rethrow. An Error could leave the
recorder as the InterruptedException a sneaky Trail.add await throws, and
Parking:49 would park it -- against D-098, and against D-111's own promise
that the original is rethrown unchanged. The record call now sits in its own
try. No control flow moved: the guard stays, throw failure stays last, and it
still throws the same object.

Two JDK facts shape the catch. addSuppressed(self) throws
IllegalArgumentException, so the attach is guarded on identity -- and that is
not hypothetical, because a toString() that does "throw this" makes the
recording failure the original. And addSuppressed is a silent no-op when
suppression is disabled, so the WARNING is the only report that case gets.
That WARNING deliberately carries no throwable, only its class, the message id
and the step: a handler would have to format it, and formatting is what threw
in the first place. Nothing in this catch may be able to replace the original.
The cost is the recording failure's message and stack, and the comment says so
rather than leaving it to be discovered. A log handler that itself throws
remains FIX-127's, contained node-wide rather than class by class.

Review r1 FAILed this, on the only thing that could have mattered. Narrowing
the new catch from Throwable back to RuntimeException left all four cases
green, because every one of them made recording fail with an
IllegalStateException. The widening that IS the fix was untested -- FIX-133's
defect reintroduced one method deeper, in the commit that was meant to close
that family. Two cases now pin it: a toString() that sneaky-throws a checked
InterruptedException, and one that throws a StackOverflowError. The second is
not redundant; under catch (Exception) it is the only one that dies.

r1's second reason was acceptance evidence that my own instruction destroyed.
I told the implementer to overwrite the green log, which erased the unfiltered
module run it had already done, so the reviewer could not confirm it from disk
and said so instead of taking my word. Logs are appended now, section by
section.

All five of the reviewer's mutants were run by the verifier and killed, with
each mutated line quoted back from the file before its run and cmp clean after
each restore. One result corrects both of us: dropping the identity guard is
killed ONLY by the self-suppression fixture. The reviewer predicted case 1
would kill it too and I agreed; we were both wrong, because every other case's
recording failure is a different object, so addSuppressed succeeds with or
without the guard. That single case is the entire guard against an
IllegalArgumentException leaving in place of the original.

Evidence: RecorderTest 20/20, flottila-core 517 tests with zero failures,
errors and skips, and the integrated gate run shared with FIX-139 -- clean
verify -Pit exit 0, BUILD SUCCESS, 12:00, 17 modules SUCCESS, every Tests run
line at zero, no blocked platform thread, coverage "no decrease" with core at
99.6 line and 99.5 branch.

